{"name":"Heat of Summer","hex":"#e98d5b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"heat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Thermal energy.","Heating system; a system that raises the temperature of a room or building."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause an increase in temperature of (an object or space); to cause to become hot .","To become hotter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heat"},{"term":"summer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of four seasons, traditionally the second, marked by the longest and typically hottest days of the year due to the inclination of the Earth and thermal lag. Typically regarded as spanning  either the period between the summer solstice to the autumnal equinox, or the months of June, July, and August in the Northern Hemisphere and the months of December, January and February in the Southern Hemisphere.","year; used to give the age of a person, usually a young one."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spend the summer, as in a particular place on holiday."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/summer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Heat","blocks":[{"text":"In thermodynamics, heat is defined as energy in transfer between a body and its surroundings, other than through thermodynamic work or through transfer of matter."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Glowing_metal.jpg/330px-Glowing_metal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heat","wikidata":"Q44432"},{"title":"Summer","blocks":[{"text":"Summer or summertime is the hottest and brightest of the four temperate seasons, occurring after spring and before autumn. Starting on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. In the Northern Hemisphere, summer is usually defined as starting on June 21 or 22, and ending on September 22 or 23; and in the Southern Hemisphere, as the period between December 22 or 23 and March 20 or 21."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer","wikidata":"Q1313"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Portrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg/250px-Portrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Portrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Portrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg/250px-Portrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Galileo Galilei","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_Galileo_Galilei.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/John_Locke._Portrait_by_Herman_Verelst_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-John_Locke._Portrait_by_Herman_Verelst_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/John_Locke._Portrait_by_Herman_Verelst_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-John_Locke._Portrait_by_Herman_Verelst_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John Locke","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_Locke._Portrait_by_Herman_Verelst_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Brook_Taylor_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Brook_Taylor_%28croppe